course: everyday
lesson: 514
title: Dementia and the first death in the family
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Dementia is an illness of the brain that makes people forget, more and more over time, even the people they love; Alzheimer's is the most common kind. In Leeds, Holly's Nan calls her «Sarah», which is Mum's name, then asks, «Who are you, love?» «It's me, Nan. Holly.» Nan smiles kindly, but doesn't know. Dementia often wipes the newest memories first, and the oldest last, so Nan may be seeing little Sarah from forty years ago. It is the illness, not Nan's choice, and she can still feel love even when the names are gone.
explain: In Edinburgh, Grandad tells Torquil the same story about the ferry three times in one visit. «Ye telt me, Grandad» (Scots for you told me). Mum gives Torquil a look. Pointing it out only upsets someone with dementia; listening as if it's the first time makes them happy. In Melbourne, Nana suddenly shouts at Lettie, «Get out, you thief, that's my purse!» Lettie runs out crying. Dementia can change moods too; it's the illness talking, not Nana. Lettie can tell a grown-up, and keep her distance when she needs to. In Toronto, Marisol visits Grandma in a care home that smells of disinfectant and gravy; Grandma is sleepy, and Marisol doesn't want to go back. Short visits with a drawing or a song are fine.
explain: Then one day someone dies. In Bristol, Wilf goes to his first funeral for his grandad. Everyone's crying, and he isn't, and he feels proper weird, like something's wrong with him. Nothing is. People grieve in different ways and at different times; tears might come weeks later, or not at all. In Glasgow, Elsie didn't get to say goodbye to her granny in hospital, so she writes her a letter and leaves it on the grave. «I feel a wee bit better» (wee is Scots for little). Finding your own way to say goodbye helps.
explain: Grief can be shared. In Ohio, after Grandpa dies, Jem's mom is sad all the time, so one Sunday he makes her breakfast in bed: burnt toast and very strong tea. She cries, then hugs him for ages. In Birmingham, Aziza's Nani dies in Pakistan. The funeral happens the next day, as Muslim funerals usually happen very quickly, and the family can't travel in time. They pray at home together, and Aziza looks at Nani's photo. In Cork, at Granda's wake (a gathering at home to remember the person, often with the coffin there), people tell funny stories: «He was some craic» (Irish for great fun). Fintan is surprised people are laughing, then laughs too. Laughing can be part of grieving.
explain: After a death, many children start to worry. In Kent, Olly, 9, whose grandma has just died, asks at bedtime, «Will you die, Mum?» Mum holds his hand: «One day, but not for a very, very long time, and I'm healthy, and I'm right here.» Olly feels better. Asking the scary question out loud is brave, and talking helps.
fact: Dementia is an illness of the brain that makes people forget, more and more over time; Alzheimer's disease is the most common kind.
fact: Dementia often affects the newest memories first, so a person may remember long ago better than this morning.
fact: People with dementia often repeat themselves; listening kindly is better than saying «you told me».
fact: Dementia can change a person's mood, making them angry or scared for no clear reason; it is the illness, not the person's choice.
fact: A person with dementia can still feel love and enjoy company, even when they forget names.
fact: Care homes can feel strange to children; short visits with a drawing or a song are fine.
fact: People grieve in different ways; not crying at a funeral doesn't mean you don't care.
fact: Grief can come and go for a long time, and it usually slowly gets easier.
fact: Writing a letter, looking at photos or praying can help someone say goodbye.
fact: When a family can't travel to a funeral, they can remember the person together at home.
fact: In many Muslim families the funeral and burial happen very quickly, often within a day.
fact: At an Irish wake people gather to remember the person, and sad and funny stories are both part of it.
fact: After a death, many children worry that someone else they love will die; asking and talking helps.
fact: «Telt» is Scots for told; «wee» means little; «craic» is Irish for fun; «proper weird» means really weird.
exercise: What does dementia do? | Makes people forget, more and more | Makes people grow taller | Makes people catch colds easily | Makes people lose their sight, a bit at a time
exercise: Nan called Holly «Sarah». Whose name is that? | Mum's | Holly's cousin's | The cat's | Nan's sister's
exercise: Can Nan still feel love even when she forgets Holly's name? | Yes | No, love goes when names go | Only on her good days, never on bad ones | Only for people she met last week
exercise: What is the most common kind of dementia? | Alzheimer's disease | Chickenpox | Hay fever | A bad cold that won't go away
exercise: Grandad told the same story three times. What's the kindest thing for Torquil to do? | Listen as if it's the first time | Say «You told me that already, Grandad» | Leave the room each time he starts | Tell him the end so he can stop
exercise: Why did Nana shout «you thief» at Lettie? | Dementia was changing her mood | Lettie had really taken the purse | Nana had stopped loving her | Lettie had been very rude to her
exercise: What can Lettie do when Nana gets angry? | Tell a grown-up and step away if she needs | Shout «thief» back at her even louder | Never visit Nana again, not even at Christmas | Hide Nana's purse to teach her a lesson
exercise: Marisol doesn't want to go back to the care home. What's a good idea? | A short visit with a drawing or a song | Never go again, Grandma won't notice | Stay for a whole day without a break | Ask Grandma to come home on the bus with her
exercise: Wilf didn't cry at the funeral. What is true? | People grieve in different ways | He mustn't have loved his grandad | Something is wrong with him | He should pretend to cry
exercise: How did Elsie say goodbye to her granny? | A letter left on the grave | A song on the radio | A balloon from hospital | A phone call to Granny's friend
exercise: What did Jem do for his sad mom? | Made her breakfast in bed | Told her to cheer up | Left her alone for a week | Bought her a new car
exercise: Why couldn't Aziza's family go to Nani's funeral in Pakistan? | It happened the next day, too soon to travel | They weren't invited by the rest of the family | They didn't want to go to Pakistan again | Nani had asked them to stay in Birmingham
exercise: What is a wake? | A gathering to remember the person | A party for waking up early | A boat race on a river | A church service for new babies and their families
exercise: What does «He was some craic» mean? | He was great fun | He was very cracked | He was always crying | He was a bit mean
exercise: Olly asks, «Will you die, Mum?» What did Mum say? | «One day, but not for a very, very long time» | «Don't be silly, I'll live forever» | «Stop asking, go to sleep» | «I don't know, maybe quite soon, who can tell»
test: Holly's Nan, who has dementia, remembers her wedding 50 years ago but not what she had for breakfast. Why? | Dementia often wipes the newest memories first | She's pretending to forget breakfast | Old memories are always forgotten first | She never ate breakfast that day
test: Nan was born in 1950 and Mum (Sarah) in 1980. Holly is 10 now, in 2026, and Nan calls her «Sarah». In which year was Mum the age Holly is now? | 1990 | 2016 | 1980 | 1960
test: Torquil, annoyed: «He's told me the ferry story like three times, it's long, man». What would most likely happen if he said «You told me!» every time? | Grandad would get upset or confused | Grandad's memory would get better | Grandad would thank him for keeping count | Nothing at all would happen
test: Wilf's mate: «you didn't even cry at your grandad's funeral, that's well cold». What's true? | Not crying doesn't mean you don't care | Not crying means you're heartless | Everyone must cry at a funeral | He should have cried on purpose so people saw
test: In a film, the girl cries once at the funeral and is totally fine the next day. What's truer in real life? | Grief can come and go for a long time | Grief always ends after one day | Children don't feel grief at all | Only grown-ups cry at funerals, children never
test: At the wake, Fintan's cousin laughs at a story about Granda. Fintan: «that's dead rude, he's dead!» What's the truth? | Laughing at happy memories is part of grieving | Laughing at a wake is always rude | Nobody laughs at Irish wakes | The cousin didn't love Granda one bit
test: Lettie: «Nana called me a thief, she hates me now». What's truer? | It's the illness, not Nana's real feelings | Nana really thinks Lettie is a thief | Nana has always secretly hated her | Lettie must have done something wrong to her
test: Olly can't sleep: «what if you die tomorrow??» What's the most helpful thing for him to do? | Ask Mum and talk about it | Keep the worry secret | Stay awake all night guarding | Google scary things
test: Aziza is sad she missed Nani's funeral. Which is a good way to remember Nani? | Praying and looking at her photo with family | Forgetting Nani as quickly as possible | Pretending Nani is still alive and well in Pakistan | Never mentioning Nani again at home
test: Jem: «Mom's been sad for weeks since Grandpa died, is she gonna be sad forever?» What's truer? | Grief can last a long time and slowly gets easier | She'll be sad every single day forever | She'll be fine by tomorrow morning | Jem made her sad and should keep out of her way
test: Marisol says the care home «lowkey smells» (lowkey means a bit, quietly). What matters most about the visit? | Grandma enjoys seeing her | How the corridor smells | How long the visit lasts | What's for dinner in the care home that night
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises dementia? | An illness of the brain causing progressive loss of memory | A wilful neglect by the sufferer to remember others | A condition caused by insufficient effort on the sufferer's part | A brief illness from which recovery is swift
exam: Identify the most appropriate response of a child to a grandparent with dementia who repeats the same anecdote. | To listen attentively as though hearing it for the first time | To remind the grandparent repeatedly of the prior telling | To withdraw from the room on each occasion | To correct each and every inaccuracy in the grandparent's account
exam: Ascertain the correct interpretation of a child's absence of tears at the funeral of a grandparent. | It implies nothing as to the child's affection | It demonstrates indifference towards the deceased | It requires a remedy by the feigning of tears | It indicates a disorder requiring medical attention
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning anxiety in children following a bereavement. | Children often fear further deaths, and discussion helps | Children never experience such anxiety whatsoever | Such fears ought always to be concealed from parents | Such fears are a sign of disobedience that ought to be punished
